# id  tag  kind  t_s  alt_km  L_si_W  flag  note
1 - first_light 0.0030 100.000 4.3124e+03 info  L_si first exceeds 1e-6 of its peak. This is just a visibility threshold; the real value depends on your sensor floor and on where the simulation starts (zstart).
2 A ablation_onset 2.1360 82.294 9.8688e+04 stylized  Net heating flux first exceeds radiative loss at T_ablate = 2300 K, so ablation switches on (a kink in the slope). Real onset is gradual over a few km. BELOW this point the model's light is drag work only (no ablated material): treat light above ~82 km as low-confidence. Loosely like the end of Phase 1 / start of Phase 2 in [R22], but the model has no spin, volatile (Na/K) emission or melt physics.
3 Y first_yield 4.7970 60.362 5.1643e+06 physical  Loading rho v^2 reaches S0 = 0.08 MPa. The body starts to flatten (pancake): area, drag and heating all grow, so the light rises faster. Analogue of the onset of fragmentation (Phase 5 / H_f in [R22]).
4 F flare 4.8394 60.014 1.3721e+07 stylized  Light rises x5.6 in 0.15 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 0 at 60.3 km (99% of m0); split gen 0 at 59.7 km (99% of m0). Dust groups supply 41% of the rise and 34% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
5 B first_breakup 4.8690 59.772 2.3595e+07 stylized  Spread factor reached f_split = 2: the body breaks into 4 children plus 20 % of its mass as dust. The dust has a large area-to-mass ratio, decelerates and ablates within a fraction of a second, which makes a sharp flare. The instantaneous release is a model simplification. Light grew x4.6 between first yield and this breakup (pancake spreading).
6 F flare 7.1570 41.842 1.8480e+08 stylized  Light rises x2.5 in 0.10 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 1 at 41.9 km (29% of m0); yield gen 1 at 41.9 km (14% of m0); yield gen 1 at 41.9 km (10% of m0) +5 more. Dust groups supply 15% of the rise and 16% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
7 P peak 7.1743 41.706 2.8041e+08 physical  Maximum light. It sits ~+1.0 km from the peak of energy deposition (40.8 km) because the luminous efficiency tau(v) falls as the fragments slow (mass-weighted speed here 14.3 km/s).
8 - drag_overtakes_ablation 7.2043 41.498 2.3645e+08 stylized  Drag work now exceeds the kinetic energy carried off by ablated mass. The ratio ablation/drag is about Lambda v^2/(2 Q Cd), so this happens when the speed falls below ~14 km/s (from the model's heating law).
9 D rolloff 7.5103 39.542 1.0227e+08 physical  Light has fallen to 1/e of the peak 0.34 s after it. Cause: the kinetic energy is being used up (speed 73% of v0, 54% of the mass still in flight) and tau(v) drops with speed. Analogue of Phase 7 (decline until dark flight) in [R22].
10 T tail 9.6763 30.639 2.7922e+05 low-confidence  Below 1e-3 of the peak the light is controlled by groups being retired when they slow below v_dark = 3.0 km/s and by the tau taper (2-4 km/s). Do not interpret structure below this level.
